INT. FLOODED CHAPEL - NIGHT

Black water to the pews' seat-backs. A shattered rose window lets moonlight lay a ragged pattern on the surface. Candles in sconces burn, though nothing should light them. The altar rises like an island.

BROTHER ANSELM (50s, cassock hitched at the waist, a lantern in one hand) wades down the nave. BROTHER THADDEUS (50s, same cassock, same lantern) wades behind him. Between them, floating, a coffin.

THADDEUS
Slowly. Slowly.

ANSELM
I am going slowly.

The coffin bumps a pew. Water laps. Anselm reaches for it. Thaddeus catches the handle at the same moment. They steady it between them.

THADDEUS
He's heavier than he should be.

ANSELM
He's wet.

THADDEUS
He was never wet. We kept him in the crypt.

Anselm pauses. He turns his lantern on the coffin lid. A dark stain is seeping up through the wood.

ANSELM
That's from the flood.

THADDEUS
That's from the inside.

A low rhythmic knock begins within the coffin. Not violent. Patient. Anselm lets go. Thaddeus does not.

ANSELM
Brother. Release it.

THADDEUS
(whisper)
It's him.

ANSELM
It isn't.

THADDEUS
It's the abbot. He's calling.

ANSELM
Thaddeus, the abbot was buried in the spring.

THADDEUS
Then who's knocking?

The knocking stops. In the silence, water drips from the broken window. Thaddeus lifts the lid half an inch. Anselm lunges and slams it shut. The two struggle; the lantern tips from Anselm's hand and hisses out in the water.

Darkness. Only the candles. The knocking begins again, fast now, frantic, from the altar behind them.

ANSELM (CONT'D)
(whisper)
That isn't the coffin.

They turn. On the altar, a figure kneels, face hidden, hands clasped, water running from its habit. It lifts its head. Anselm and Thaddeus freeze. It has no face at all, only smooth pale skin.

THADDEUS
Dear God.

ANSELM
(shaking)
He answers.

The figure rises, dripping, and holds out a hand to each of them. Behind them, the coffin lid opens with a groan.

THADDEUS
Brother. What do we do?

ANSELM
We kneel.

They kneel, waist-deep in black water, as the candles flare high. The figure steps down from the altar and walks across the surface of the flood.

THADDEUS
(praying, trembling)
Our Father, who art in heaven...

The figure stops before him and lays its hand on his head. Anselm squeezes his eyes shut. A long exhale, as of a very old furnace.

ANSELM
(whisper)
Hallowed be thy name.

The candles go out, one by one.

Silence. Then the soft knocking begins again, and from every pew, a dozen more hands rise up through the water to answer.
